Electron coupling to a waveguide mode (Cruz + Leila)

project overleaf: https://www.overleaf.com/7456489821nzcssmvbqncb#9faea3

This repository contains Python and C++ code for simulating and analyzing Electron Energy Loss Spectroscopy (EELS) in
waveguide structures. The project includes both analytical and numerical approaches (BEM simulations) to compute the
EELS response under various physical conditions.
========================================================================================================================

.- /figures: schemes and figures

.- /permittivities: data of real materials, permittivities from "Refractive Index" (Si and Ge)

.- global_constants.py: define universal constants to import in all codes 

.- permittivity_epsilon.py: define \epsilon(ω) of different materials

.- EELS.py: define functions for the EELS from Eq. 3 #paper 228, Eq. 25 paper 149.
this is defined per unit length. Later we define an effective length propagation Leff
and we integrate over the electron's trajectory.
========================================================================================================================
FIG 1: Guided Modes and EELS per Unit Length
========================================================================================================================
.- plot_EELS_per_unit_length.py: see the guide modes for different Ee (electron energy)
   plot the dEELS/dy (EELS per unit length, not integrated over e- trajectory)
	- plot the EELS(k_parallel,ω) to find the waveguide modes of
		- real materials. import function \epsilon_2(ω)
		- fake materials adding Im(epsi_2) = 0.1 to make the EELS wider and see it better
	- plot the EELS(ze,ω) integrated over k_parallel. 
		- ze is an important parameter and it determinates which modes are being excited
========================================================================================================================
 FIG 2 : EELS Integrated Using Leff(k_parallel)
========================================================================================================================
.- plot_EELS_integrated_over_kx.py : EELS's spectrum using Leff.
   plot EELS integrated over the y-coordinate by using Leff(k_par)=L0*f(k_par) and integrating over kx from 0 to infty
         - plot EELS(ω)/L0 vs ω. ---> Fig. 2b
	
.- plot_EELS_integrated_over_kx_and_omega.py : total EELS.
   plot EELS integrated over the y-coordinate using Leff(k_par), over k_parallel, and over ω from 0 to ω_f
  
   this double integral takes too much time. the integral limits of k_par depend on ω because I integrate over k_x/k
   with k = ω/c. In addition, epsilon2(ω) depends on energy so is a double integral. 
         - plot EELS(ω_f)/L0 vs ω_f ---> Fig. 2c
         - plot EELS(ω_f)/L0 vs ω_f normalized to the integral of ω from 0 to infty ---> Fig. 2d
   		 
Approximate the integrals as a sum always
   
========================================================================================================================
FIG 3 : Potential and BEM Simulations OF A RECTANGULAR WAVEGUIDE
========================================================================================================================
.- /potential : A) potential of a 3 nanowires with boundary conditions elements using "e.h" and "e.cpp" to
   execute it 
    
    	 - EELS_integrated.py : define functions: 
    	 	- run_dy_out: run the c++ code that defines the potential V(z)/V0. Important to change the sign!
    	 	- EELS_from_BEM_interpolated: interpolation of EELS from BEM as a function of z/width
    	 	- P_integrand_over_z: combine EELS and potential results to construct P(ω,z) defined in 
    	 	"Eq_P_integral_over_z.png"
    	 	- P_integrated_over_z: same as before integrated over z/width
    	 	- find_width_of_peak: find the width of a function with a peak from fitting with a Lorentzian.
    	 	This is use to integrate the EELS over the mode/peak (over energy)
    	 
         - plot_bmin_vs_V0_theta.py : finds the bmin above the waveguide only (we want the electron above the wg). This
         executes dy.cpp, with zmin above the surface of the wg. Plots color map of bmin(θ, V0) extracting the (θ, V0)
         values for a fixed b_min to use later for the integration of EELS 
         ----------> Generates Fig. 3a: b_min(θ, V0) + data for Fig. 3b
         
         - plot_potential_overlap_Eerror.py : calculates deltaE (see comments GM 2025/06/19 + 2025/06/20). 
         Calculates the beams divergency   
         ----------> Generates Fig. SI?: potential V(z) and the beams divergency for the parameters we use
         
         - plot_potential_different_parameters.py : makes extra plots of V(x,z) to understand the behaviour, 
         as a function of d/W and as a function of h/W (ratio) ... etc
         
         - plot_potential_vs_comsol.py : comparing the results of the new potential (3 nanowires) with the results from
         Comsol from Saad
         
         - plot_EELS_integrated_over_z_1mode_vs_theta.py :
          
         	1) Use the BEM2D files for EELS per unit length per unit eV to integrated the trajectory of the electron
         	(from zmin(theta,V0) to infty) in combination with dz/V(z)... from c++ code (see definition written in
         	"Eq_P_integral_over_z.png") and uses the data from Fig. 3a
         	----------> Generates Fig. 3b: EELS (1/eV) vs photon energy
         	
         	2) Integration of Fig. 3b around the mode j as a function of θ for a fixed value of zmin (contour_level)
         	We fit the width of the mode N for every V0,θ and using the width we integrate the EELS over energy as
         	a function of (V0,θ) for a fixed b_min
         	----------> Generates data for Fig. 3c: total EELS vs θ/V0 for diff values of bmin or for diff modes (peaks)
    
         - plot_tot_EELS_vs_theta.py : 
         ----------> Use data to plot Fig. 3c: total EELS vs θ/V0 for diff values of bmin or for diff modes (peaks)
         
         - plot_EELS_integrated_1mode_vs_h.py :
         
                1) Use the BEM2D files for EELS per unit length per unit eV to integrated around the mode j vs h/W
                and to find the frequency of the modes vs h/W. Similar to "plot_EELS_integrated_over_z_1mode_vs_theta.py"
                without the integration over z and as a function of h/W. Here the potential is not involved, only with 
                data from BEM2D. 
         
         - c++ codes: to run everything in sonic, without the need of dowloading things 
                - e.h and e.cpp and e.out : new potential with 3 wires (two positives and 1 negative, see figures/scheme/scheme_plane3.png)
                - split_eels2.h : split the EELS data in different frequencies 
                - EELS_integrated_over_z.h : integration of the EELS over z
                - EELS_integrated_over_z.cpp : execute the EELS_integrated_over_z.h
                      
                HAS TO BE DONE: 
      		- .sh to split the EELS in frequencies
      		- .sh to run the potential and save the data 
      		- .sh to run the potential at z=h,x=0 to normalize the V to V0
      		- .cpp of EELS_integrated with loading the data from c++ (similar fuction as BEM files)
      
                
                
.- /bem2D : B) EELS of ribbon (rectangular waveguide) made of Si with extra loss for different values of impact
   parameter (distance between the electron and the 0,0 placed at the center of the waveguide) 
	
	    -. /geometry : check the geometry of the rectangular waveguide
	    -. /datfiles_EELS : check the convergency of the EELS for different N 
	    
	    - plot_EELS.py : check the convergency of the EELS as a function of N 
	    
	    - plot_EELS_vs_h.py : EELS as a function of W*\omega/c for different h/W to see how the peaks change. 
	    
	    - plot_field_electron_position.py : maps of the EELS 
	    
	    - genbemax files + run: run in BEM2D the EELS for a fixed point "xe" for many values of h, for Ee = (100, 200)keV,
	    for W = (300,400,500) nm. 
	    
	    	- genbemax_EELS_rect_wg_sonic: for "xe=0"
	    	- genbemax_EELS_rect_wg_sonic1: for "xe=W*0.4"
	    	- genbemax_EELS_rect_wg_sonic2: for "xe=W*0.8"
	    	- run_EELS_rect_wg
	    
